#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d4c1a7b-2341-5b8c-8112-0290f8eda65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1cc03cef-e717-5ac1-ab9d-75508f3aa056" :question/math-academy-id "e-5533" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/7014ce766be779f4231c78a99a6e013bfc48bfd9e21da7fa565c49e466437c99.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5e03c481312af462a0739b4670e24325d2e06cc10449c65bfb04208206fc5d58.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/712c3f663a367090ab5ba81705e7c7f36ce6d4e2d6e2b13cc59383421e408ea7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" :question/math-academy-id "e-5534" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/31a6051588cdb215a025179138803257d01f72cb2f231cda05cbbd67b56b0fb9.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/2a08ff9b84de36c2b9141809f385f688f6b8c83b2b916bd3469432705636a154.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/50b840579f0396b87913b773975c26b337c9846c139bc4a1bf9445984681f062.png)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/55b4ecb56daf7f8cce19878af9f17e237a5f38b1593a4000d39487cb93ff2c95.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2a7fbc-8911-507f-915c-cf9b50653e5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5535" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5fd08491c00587e0efa4017850407c58af8ad0821c2c1aab512ec20b5295d128.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find a possible matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4},$ \n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/c8b3063b226f25c53f064e3f427446da7cc4c7cd99a20b8c1d9e46d33bd429e0.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/0489fb7ddf3f73682e4e61345fa73a601c5f8ae01bf319918becd9c2426cc73d.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/350a5319ff61e3f794e780561616c7a9523f18832ee040ce8faa0825818f8f24.png)\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}.$ Therefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/c6213ccfec2acbc449e38e431ef1d60d2e06b19066b735faa6426dfd4b326c9c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/id #uuid "da515ea7-f4b0-58c5-adab-c826563dd3cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5906" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/139905ee461ed3a946aa232e42e65f619f1550884b24dc72354303e3af7a05df.png)\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/66747ae8628c72623aa51610ed39af91154d06d61e67189f93d04079b64f494a.png)\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/eef90c4d866110a74a221ce82ad275de5b07ddbf6e33213d94cf5f7831295022.png)\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}.$\n\n- From our matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5449e99fabebae1514f40bdf40698e84bb60a578e495e5a363ed7ac617749ad2.png)\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/cdf7fb1cb310f63e0dcf468aa045dc0fb1bb3b2e757ae431ae499c898ab5538f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e21ce7b2-f61a-52d3-b21b-616e1077fa6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372863} {:answer/id #uuid "88dd830f-795c-5d61-a5ba-ac9819e8d8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372864} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb38f54-0cb0-5a82-a42d-07299bd2dd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372865} {:answer/id #uuid "21f32250-f116-5a50-a8e4-654bc34f0c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866} {:answer/id #uuid "9814f43e-8bbf-5e54-bfcb-59bfbc700347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb38f54-0cb0-5a82-a42d-07299bd2dd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372865} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d39770-bd9f-52cd-8e14-d2f091e4da2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db2db2cc-8132-5f94-95c8-6b73da358424" :question/math-academy-id "q-135440" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c23340-a540-58a1-88bc-1dcb2916e663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372870} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc5066a-1afb-58f5-855a-3646c81acc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-5\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i} \\\\ 3∓5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372871} {:answer/id #uuid "633c5c28-afe1-5d78-a3c4-05f720f334f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872} {:answer/id #uuid "26020a13-3c8f-5fa0-9bed-55389655a45f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372873} {:answer/id #uuid "e38de174-6f9e-553b-8dd1-2c765a5a62e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4∓6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "633c5c28-afe1-5d78-a3c4-05f720f334f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872} :answer-field/id #uuid "923f081c-200e-5d55-8514-b85f063558a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f379e30-572f-57a1-9150-93e8b682cd6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135441" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :know