es 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9ea343-2e8f-52c1-acfd-83529ac270f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372954} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d28d20-f44b-5605-8c9c-e2569eda1d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372955} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b64ab-cdf6-5681-9364-40b7ae6927a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372956} {:answer/id #uuid "0a622e59-6a39-53f8-a80d-584f48f852a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372957} {:answer/id #uuid "516d5753-600e-500f-937b-7fad3d62d805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b64ab-cdf6-5681-9364-40b7ae6927a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372956} :answer-field/id #uuid "235a3608-14cf-563c-bcb4-dbc1dcaf522a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7453494-e18a-50f5-85b0-d29344213529" :question/math-academy-id "q-135641" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4+\\text{i} \\\\ 2+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd425493-a8e8-50b2-8f36-cc9ab3be0957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372961} {:answer/id #uuid "74248c3b-28e0-5521-895c-22bccfc31e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372962} {:answer/id #uuid "242b1c36-7fb4-5187-a657-3719495e92b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372963} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cce2e5-b8f7-5b3d-abed-42dd03bf93db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372964} {:answer/id #uuid "7dee7eca-4054-5a7f-a7a8-5e986e8925e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1cce2e5-b8f7-5b3d-abed-42dd03bf93db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372964} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f0559f-574f-5821-9027-4685c5d7e469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fda50e7-a978-5dc2-9fa4-56049b6e6862" :question/math-academy-id "q-135660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1d348c-304b-5e31-9a59-8ca7c99a61d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372968} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b4d86a-041a-5c3b-adb0-e8596905d0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372969} {:answer/id #uuid "44a6c9f1-58d7-536c-904e-0e663e788713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372970} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6edb66-0e99-56a5-bb39-a751f2cb8abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372971} {:answer/id #uuid "bf90c121-b04c-53b7-b963-79bfcc6ce8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa1d348c-304b-5e31-9a59-8ca7c99a61d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372968} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f9d2dce-c6da-5357-8763-5690f3a25477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2aef9402-e7a5-51fc-b3a5-92703ff2805a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135662" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -6+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4+8\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9a9f274-4c8e-50ff-beaf-d7afca6cca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372975} {:answer/id #uuid "3911eb05-df8e-5a6c-a483-891ef514893d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372976} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b0466c-5884-5006-81e0-1db8cf27d169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372977} {:answer/id #uuid "0107300c-3da0-5aee-b801-15134db85106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372978} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff77e3-d88b-5bf0-9823-56588485f02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cff77e3-d88b-5bf0-9823-56588485f02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372979} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed45cff-1546-5f22-b2f8-830c6667b653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c740c6dd-218d-50dd-b80f-31fd64e4db75" :question/math-academy-id "q-135667" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa2a88c-ee4e-5552-800a-8a6f56286ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127539} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d262e2-54b6-58ca-8918-35bbc90f5311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127540} {:answer/id #uuid "34076a5c-5158-5d58-8dcf-3e7a0a7211cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127541} {:answer/id #uuid "4136254b-a043-564b-b64f-714354cf87df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127542} {:answer/id #uuid "662b5a3a-2801-529f-af8f-ebf574b450e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662b5a3a-2801-529f-af8f-ebf574b450e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9678063e-8cb2-50fb-b9c5-b7e752c39105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97813176-8f27-5690-98e4-6d6c62e9f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473d4a1b-ee56-54af-acb1-2bbdeea6317a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\mp 2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\mp \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372981} {:answer/id #uuid "ae59367b-d124-511b-9fa8-7775bbabe9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372982} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0f4e40-dfe5-585b-ad51-504658ebaccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\pm 2\\text{ } \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372983} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b96048-ed89-56b7-aec6-7a81987a7974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372984} {:answer/id #uuid "d24f52d2-2062-5454-9c62-7673e618d7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "473d4a1b-ee56-54af-acb1-2bbdeea6317a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\mp 2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\mp \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372981} :answer-field/id #uuid "e56e4edc-b065-5a13-8418-ae6113c00a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "582f937c-6cfb-53b4-966a-6cea9b2086e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38665" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af9e485c-573a-54d1-9adf-8edcb00e982f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372988} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8cc164-9b36-5303-ab33-4ea64a2a0a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372989} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8ded64-58b0-5e54-9ca4-86ded051bace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372990} {:answer/id #uuid "36d912b2-48cc-5759-b9ea-670536dbf64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372991} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab67dd5-68c2-5e4a-b0c4-9678ebc2a786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8cc164-9b36-5303-ab33-4ea64a2a0a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372989} :answer-field/id #uuid "c57b9cdd-307d-5547-80aa-a045392d612c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c41306a2-efa8-5b9b-8259-6f81fe0664c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38921" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1-5\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
